1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,532
A thousand AIs.

2
00:00:02,582 --> 00:00:05,983
One goal: the most famous theorem in math.

3
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:07,833
No human to lead them.

4
00:00:07,883 --> 00:00:10,423
In under thirty hours, they did it.

5
00:00:10,473 --> 00:00:14,314
Here's how they organized, and
what it changes for you.

6
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:18,649
In 1637, Pierre de Fermat
writes in a margin:

7
00:00:18,699 --> 00:00:25,915
a to the n plus b to the n never equals c to
the n, once n is greater than two.

8
00:00:25,965 --> 00:00:30,324
He says he has a proof, but the
margin is too small.

9
00:00:30,374 --> 00:00:36,340
It takes until 1995: Andrew Wiles
publishes a 129-page proof,

10
00:00:36,390 --> 00:00:40,087
so hard that checking it takes months.

11
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:45,289
In September 2026, Anthropic has Claude
rewrite this proof in Lean,

12
00:00:45,339 --> 00:00:48,870
a language where the computer
checks every step.

13
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:53,371
Dozens of agents, eleven days,
thirteen million lines.

14
00:00:53,421 --> 00:00:54,744
A first.

15
00:00:54,990 --> 00:01:00,664
On November 9, a new experiment: a
thousand Claude Fable 5.2 agents.

16
00:01:00,714 --> 00:01:03,799
One lead, nine hundred
ninety-nine helpers.

17
00:01:03,849 --> 00:01:06,149
No messaging, no internet.

18
00:01:06,199 --> 00:01:09,250
Just Git and a shared database.

19
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:16,856
In ten minutes, the lead creates five tables:
orders, who does what, notes, tasks,

20
00:01:16,906 --> 00:01:18,482
and finished work.

21
00:01:18,532 --> 00:01:22,728
Then he splits the goal into sixty
steps and forms crews.

22
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:23,666
First shock:

23
00:01:23,716 --> 00:01:28,451
the helpers judge forty-seven of the sixty
steps impossible in the time given.

24
00:01:28,501 --> 00:01:30,385
Three are already proved.

25
00:01:30,435 --> 00:01:33,383
The lead sends almost everyone
to the ten others.

26
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:35,930
In seventy minutes, they all fall.

27
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:39,495
And many "impossible" steps
get proved later.

28
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:42,565
The real wall is called Langlands-Tunnell.

29
00:01:42,615 --> 00:01:47,916
Its captain estimates: close to a million lines,
and less than a one percent chance.

30
00:01:47,966 --> 00:01:50,770
At hour eighteen, everything stops.

31
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:53,535
The humans restart from the last save.

32
00:01:53,585 --> 00:01:57,504
The lead then sends about seven hundred
fifty helpers to this wall.

33
00:01:57,554 --> 00:02:00,302
Every piece that's too hard
gets split again:

34
00:02:00,352 --> 00:02:03,909
two thousand four hundred eighteen
tasks, ten levels deep.

35
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:08,111
Hour 28.4: Langlands-Tunnell is proved.

36
00:02:08,161 --> 00:02:12,335
Hour 28.7: Fermat is checked
by the computer.

37
00:02:12,385 --> 00:02:16,310
Eleven days in September, under
thirty hours in November,

38
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:19,030
with a proof about seven times shorter.

39
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:20,765
What about safety?

40
00:02:20,815 --> 00:02:24,614
Each agent runs in its own
machine, with no internet.

41
00:02:24,664 --> 00:02:28,598
An automatic monitor checks every
command before it runs.

42
00:02:28,648 --> 00:02:32,540
Over a million actions: none was blocked.

43
00:02:32,790 --> 00:02:35,928
Careful: the AI did not
discover the proof.

44
00:02:35,978 --> 00:02:38,439
Wiles found it thirty years ago.

45
00:02:38,489 --> 00:02:42,376
It rewrote it so a computer can check it.

46
00:02:42,426 --> 00:02:45,514
Anthropic itself calls it a
toy experiment.

47
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:49,909
The takeaway for you: even a thousand
AIs give up too early.

48
00:02:49,959 --> 00:02:52,642
The fix works with just one, too.

49
00:02:52,692 --> 00:02:55,284
Split your big task into small ones.

50
00:02:55,334 --> 00:03:00,217
And when the AI tells you
"impossible", ask it to split again.

51
00:03:00,267 --> 00:03:02,391
The sources are in the description.

52
00:03:02,441 --> 00:03:04,141
Subscribe for what's next.

